Starting at the top-right value gives two useful directions. Moving left makes the value smaller, while moving down makes it larger. Each step removes one row or one column from consideration.

For example, with `target = 11`, if the current value is `7`, moving down is safe because everything to the left in that row is even smaller. If the current value is `16`, moving left is the useful move.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    bool searchMatrix(vector<vector<int>> &mat, int target){
        if (mat.empty() || mat[0].empty()) {
            return false;
        }

        int rows = static_cast<int>(mat.size());
        int cols = static_cast<int>(mat[0].size());
        int row = 0;
        int col = cols - 1;

        while (row < rows && col >= 0) {
            if (mat[row][col] == target) {
                return true;
            }

            if (mat[row][col] < target) {
                row++;
            } else {
                col--;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```

This does not change the input matrix. The walk takes `O(m + n)` time and `O(1)` extra space.

Another way is to use the row order only to skip rows that cannot contain the target. For every remaining row, binary search is used instead of checking its values one by one.

If a row ends before the target, later rows are checked. If a row starts after the target, later rows can be ignored because all their values are even larger. This is not as fast as one search over the virtual flattened array, but the row logic is quite straightforward.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    bool searchMatrix(vector<vector<int>> &mat, int target){
        if (mat.empty() || mat[0].empty()) {
            return false;
        }

        for (const vector<int>& row : mat) {
            if (target < row.front()) {
                return false;
            }
            if (target > row.back()) {
                continue;
            }

            int left = 0;
            int right = static_cast<int>(row.size()) - 1;

            while (left <= right) {
                int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

                if (row[middle] == target) {
                    return true;
                }
                if (row[middle] < target) {
                    left = middle + 1;
                } else {
                    right = middle - 1;
                }
            }

            return false;
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```

For a matrix with `m` rows and `n` columns, the worst-case time is `O(m log n)`. The code uses `O(1)` extra space and does not modify the input.

This keeps the two parts separate. First, the row boundaries show which row could contain the target. After that row is found, a normal binary search checks its columns.

The boundary checks also handle values smaller than the first matrix value or larger than the last one without touching an invalid row. An empty matrix returns `false` before any indexing happens.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean searchMatrix(int[][] mat, int target) {
        if (mat == null || mat.length == 0 || mat[0].length == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        int rowLeft = 0;
        int rowRight = mat.length - 1;
        int selectedRow = -1;

        while (rowLeft <= rowRight) {
            int middleRow = rowLeft + (rowRight - rowLeft) / 2;
            int first = mat[middleRow][0];
            int last = mat[middleRow][mat[middleRow].length - 1];

            if (target < first) {
                rowRight = middleRow - 1;
            } else if (target > last) {
                rowLeft = middleRow + 1;
            } else {
                selectedRow = middleRow;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (selectedRow == -1) {
            return false;
        }

        int left = 0;
        int right = mat[selectedRow].length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
            int value = mat[selectedRow][middle];

            if (value == target) {
                return true;
            }
            if (value < target) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle - 1;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

The row search costs `O(log m)` and the search inside the row costs `O(log n)`, so the total is `O(log m + log n)`. Extra space is `O(1)`.

A direct scan is enough here. The loops visit each value and stop as soon as the target appears. It does not use the sorted order, but the code is easy to check and also works for a one-row matrix.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean searchMatrix(int[][] mat, int target) {
        if (mat == null || mat.length == 0 || mat[0].length == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int row = 0; row < mat.length; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < mat[row].length; col++) {
                if (mat[row][col] == target) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(m * n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

